00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:15.630
So Dr. Ireland,

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:18.300
if you could just give us a
little bit about your journey

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:19.133
into ministry,

00:06:19.133 --> 00:06:21.150
I guess going all the way as
far back as you want to go,

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:24.030
but maybe starting from
your college days to here

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:26.070
and how you've gotten to this place.

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:27.400
- Well, my pleasure.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:29.370
I actually was an atheist.

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:32.045
I told people I was a scientific atheist

00:06:32.045 --> 00:06:34.080
from the teenage years,

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.060
and I started college at 16,

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:38.370
and I did my first degree in engineering,

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:39.750
mechanical engineering.

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:42.030
And then when I graduated at 20,

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:43.748
and I went into graduate school,

00:06:43.748 --> 00:06:46.740
did a master's in civil engineering,

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:48.720
and so I worked in that profession.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:49.890
But at the age of 20,

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:52.260
I had this life transforming experience

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:53.820
or series of experiences.

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:56.040
And that's where I became then,

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:58.290
a follower of Jesus Christ.

00:06:58.290 --> 00:06:59.850
I didn't even realize I was praying.

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:04.320
I just got to the end
of my rope, so to speak.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:07.093
And after graduating, I started thinking,

00:07:07.093 --> 00:07:10.530
"There must be more to life
than making a lot of money,

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:13.530
marrying a pretty wife,
having the nice house."

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:15.450
Even though I had none of those things,

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:20.010
and I remember sitting on
the edge of my dormitory bed

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:22.650
and I said, "God, if
you're real, change me."

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:24.150
And he changed me.

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:27.630
And my outlook, my worldview has changed.

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:30.090
The way I thought about
myself was changed.

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:32.340
And then I started to
build this relationship

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:33.537
with Jesus Christ.

00:07:33.537 --> 00:07:36.103
And I practiced engineering
for about six years

00:07:36.103 --> 00:07:38.267
after graduate school,

00:07:38.267 --> 00:07:43.170
and then I transitioned into
ministry, went to seminary,

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:44.550
then went on, did a PhD,

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:46.830
then did lots of post-doctoral work.

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:50.730
I'm a school guy, so yeah.

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:52.003
So that's where I was.

00:07:52.003 --> 00:07:56.010
I did my last postgraduate
degree at University of Cambridge

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:58.107
in England in social innovation,

00:07:58.107 --> 00:08:01.650
and so I'm always learning
and trying to grow.

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:04.710
- Right. Well, you're in good
company being a school guy.

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:06.300
And I know that a lot of our listeners

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:08.220
will be able to relate to that as well.

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:11.010
But even those who maybe
haven't been lifelong

00:08:11.010 --> 00:08:13.950
people in our education
spaces who are just continuing

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:15.180
to learn so many different ways,

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:17.490
so appreciate you sharing
that story as well.

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:18.840
And just to unpack a little bit,

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:20.910
I know we were talking about
your most recent degree,

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:23.190
but if you could tell our
listeners about some of the work

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:25.470
that you've done over the years

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:26.970
in terms of what you talked about

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:28.710
in terms of cultural competency,

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:30.360
because a lot of those
things are also near and dear

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:32.901
to what we've talked
about on this podcast.

00:08:32.901 --> 00:08:35.572
- My research interest
has always been diversity,

00:08:35.572 --> 00:08:39.237
more particularly building
multiracial organizations.

00:08:39.237 --> 00:08:43.200
And so when I did my
dissertation years ago,

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:44.400
about 20 years ago,

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:46.050
it was on the black-white relationship

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:49.500
in large multiracial churches
in America to understand that.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:52.170
This most recent dissertation was on,

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:57.170
how do you help white leaders
enjoy developing a lifestyle

00:08:57.390 --> 00:08:59.130
of intercultural competence?

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:01.140
That means how do you, because right now,

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:04.020
a lot of white males
particularly feel demonized

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:06.810
when it comes to having
conversations about diversity,

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:07.650
equity, and inclusion.

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:12.000
And so, I have then worked on that space.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:15.630
And so I do consulting for
some of the major corporations

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:17.207
in addition to being a pastor.

00:09:17.207 --> 00:09:22.207
I'm a pastor of a church, about
10,000 people, and has 75,

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:24.810
or I should say 70
different nationalities.

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:28.107
And so I provide leadership
to Asians, whites,

00:09:28.107 --> 00:09:31.235
Native Americans, blacks, Latinos,

00:09:31.235 --> 00:09:35.370
so the whole spectrum, so to speak,

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:36.840
and so I'm honored to do that.

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:40.200
And I say that and I give that information

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:43.860
because the racially
diverse church in America

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:45.990
is a very rare animal.

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:48.030
And for someone then to be of color

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:50.610
leading a racially diverse church,

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:54.183
that's like an Elvis
sighting, so to speak.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:59.580
I mean, it represents
less than 1%, so to speak,

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:00.413
statistically.

00:10:00.413 --> 00:10:02.820
So it's very rare, but it's
an intentional process.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:06.420
You don't bring about
diversity by happenstance.

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:09.690
There's intentionality, there's planning,

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:12.690
and there's a lot of heart
change and introspection,

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:16.200
those kinds of searching and
probative investigative things,

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:18.390
so you can be able to understand.

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:21.060
So it's almost even like
when I deal with preaching,

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:24.330
I've gone through where
you have individuals

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:26.250
who listen to your sermons,

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:29.955
be it black, white,
Asian, atheist, agnostics,

00:10:29.955 --> 00:10:31.770
and they give feedback.

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:33.930
How am I being interpreted?

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:35.460
How am I coming across?

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:36.780
What are you hearing?

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:37.613
In other words,

00:10:37.613 --> 00:10:40.590
am I speaking cross-culturally?
Am I speaking to everybody?

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:44.190
And unless we go through
that type of reflection

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:47.130
and I should say that exercise,

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:48.810
we don't know how we're coming across.

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:50.340
We don't know how we're being interpreted.

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:52.950
- Yeah. Well, I appreciate you
putting that all in context,

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:55.830
and I a hundred percent
agree in terms of the rarity

00:10:55.830 --> 00:10:58.770
of what you're doing as a
black male leader as well,

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:00.630
and how that's not often the case.

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:03.720
But it's really refreshing
to hear also how much

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:05.700
you are a learner and a listener,

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:07.320
even as you've listened to that feedback.

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:11.070
And I'd imagine that's also
facilitated your effectiveness

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:12.720
over the years in just
the different spaces

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:14.430
that God has placed you in.

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:15.263
- Absolutely.

00:11:15.263 --> 00:11:17.250
In fact, I've had the opportunity to speak

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:19.020
in some 75 countries.

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:22.320
One of the countries I remember
going to, I was in Zambia,

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:24.600
so you think that this south
central African country

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:27.630
that I'll be able to be a
winner when I get there,

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:28.991
so to speak.

00:11:28.991 --> 00:11:31.440
And I'm in Lusaka, the capital,

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:33.900
and so this is a group
of about 8,000 leaders

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:34.860
that were there.

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:38.130
And so when I started,
after my first talk,

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:41.340
I can feel there was this
gap between me and them.

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:42.997
And so I asked the host, I said,

00:11:42.997 --> 00:11:44.700
"Am I correct in my perception

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:46.500
that there's this wall between us,

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:49.800
between me and the
leaders that are there?"

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:51.660
And he said, "You're correct."

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:53.340
And I said, "Why is that there?

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:54.173
I don't know them.

00:11:54.173 --> 00:11:55.890
I have not done anything to them."

00:11:55.890 --> 00:11:56.820
And they said this to me,

00:11:56.820 --> 00:11:57.750
which I'll never forget,

00:11:57.750 --> 00:11:59.880
he said, "That because you're an American

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:01.230
and they think that
you're gonna hurt them."

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:06.230
So in order to break that wall down,

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:09.240
even though we shared the same
race, different nationality,

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:12.960
different culture, but we
had the same racial makeup,

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:16.850
both being black, the gap was,

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:19.920
or I should say, in order
for me to be successful in

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:23.460
that conversation and that
series of conference meetings,

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:25.110
I had to become more vulnerable.

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:29.190
So the more I was able to
display a common humanity,

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:30.780
that's when they dropped their guards.

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:33.630
Because they put me beforehand
in a category that says,

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:34.830
you're an American.

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:38.730
And Americans will think
that they're better than us,

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:40.920
and we are quote unquote poor Africans.

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:42.690
And so we don't want to be hurt again.

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:44.520
And so they had that predisposition

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:46.170
before I even opened my mouth.

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:47.850
And so I had to help get around that.

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:51.510
But similar things happen no
matter what country you're in,

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:54.030
you find that you have to
have those conversations.

00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:57.476
And to your point, to be a
learner, I have to ask people,

00:12:57.476 --> 00:12:59.725
"How am I being interpreted?

00:12:59.725 --> 00:13:02.250
How am I coming across?"

00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:05.250
And I must then be willing
to shift and pivot quickly,

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:08.040
so I can be engaging with
that particular audience.

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:10.497
- Hmm. Well, I really
appreciate that story

00:13:10.497 --> 00:13:12.130
and just your openness to that as well.

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:14.280
And I think that speaks
to volumes just to,

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:17.712
even the assumptions that
we carry into places,

00:13:17.712 --> 00:13:20.520
regardless of whether we intend to or not,

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:22.230
both from the perspective
of those who are listening

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:23.850
and from you coming into that place

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:25.290
and not even expecting that.

00:13:25.290 --> 00:13:28.200
So I think that's really, really powerful.

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:29.640
But as we've been talking

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:30.870
and you've been sharing again,

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:32.490
about all these different
things that you do,

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:35.360
several of which again, tie
into the podcast episode.

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:37.080
So I'm trying not to go too far astray

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:39.240
and bring us back to the
topic at hand as well,

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:40.770
but just thought it would
be helpful for our listeners

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:42.930
to kind of have that context.

00:13:42.930 --> 00:13:43.770
But it's funny,

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:45.930
as I was reading your
bio and thinking about

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:47.190
all the different things that you're doing

00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:48.630
and some of what you're sharing there

00:13:48.630 --> 00:13:50.670
and this topic about burnout,

00:13:50.670 --> 00:13:52.170
my mind immediately went to a place of,

00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:54.660
how is this person not spread too thin

00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:56.400
and all the things that they do?

00:13:56.400 --> 00:13:58.380
So I wonder if you could also
just share with our listeners

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:02.460
as well, just your structure as it were,

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:04.170
in terms of how you lead.

00:14:04.170 --> 00:14:06.840
And then also, I know we'll
get into this topic of burnout,

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:08.520
but what your experience has been,

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:10.050
either from your own personal experience

00:14:10.050 --> 00:14:12.700
or from those that you lead
on this topic of burnout?

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:15.540
- I appreciate the question,

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:17.160
and I'll be very candid with you,

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:20.850
that I would look back maybe 20 years ago,

00:14:20.850 --> 00:14:22.110
I only knew one speed.

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:27.110
I just, that's how I
lived, I was just working.

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:31.232
I tell people my love
language is productivity.

00:14:31.232 --> 00:14:34.620
The more you produce,
the more I feel loved.

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:36.838
I don't need a hug. I just need,

00:14:36.838 --> 00:14:41.310
it's almost like that line
on the Amazon website,

00:14:41.310 --> 00:14:42.583
where's my stuff?

00:14:42.583 --> 00:14:45.273
I just want my stuff.

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:50.160
But over the years, the last 20 years,

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:53.070
I've had to put in place
what I call sacred rhythms.

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:54.130
So for me,

00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:58.680
one of the things I have to do
since I'm the public speaker,

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:03.270
I'll speak no more than four
weeks in a row as a pastor,

00:15:03.270 --> 00:15:05.340
and then take two weeks off.

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:07.860
And I'm saying that because
there was a study that was done

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:10.380
years ago on the University
of Texas that said,

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:12.150
one hour of public speaking

00:15:12.150 --> 00:15:14.760
is equal to eight hours of manual labor.

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:17.010
So your body actually goes
through the physical shock,

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:19.500
like I was doing a
construction worker's job,

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:22.020
because it's very emptying, very draining.

00:15:22.020 --> 00:15:24.570
When you do that week
after week after week,

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:26.730
after a while you can
become a basket case.

00:15:26.730 --> 00:15:29.340
So thankfully I was able to then,

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:30.870
to create the sacred rhythms,

00:15:30.870 --> 00:15:32.730
you have to have effective teams.

00:15:32.730 --> 00:15:33.630
So I took time,

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:36.810
I took a step back and
started to train people,

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:39.000
individuals that were very competent,

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:40.650
they had gifts, skills.

00:15:40.650 --> 00:15:42.090
It took time to do that.

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:46.027
And you know, I remember as
the Zambian proverb that says,

00:15:46.027 --> 00:15:48.780
"When you run alone, you run fast.

00:15:48.780 --> 00:15:52.020
But when you run together, you run far."

00:15:52.020 --> 00:15:57.020
I had been in the first 20
years of ministry running fast.

00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:58.920
Now I run together.

00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:03.570
And so I slowed down, put
in place gifted leaders,

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:05.550
trained them, took the time to train them,

00:16:05.550 --> 00:16:09.120
the time to coach them and
to make sure and mentor them,

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:12.240
so that I can be able to
have these sacred rhythms.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:16.440
And then help put that structure in place

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:18.840
for themselves so they
won't experience burnout.

00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:22.320
- Yeah. That's really
powerful in a lot of ways,

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:24.450
and actually makes me think
about some of the topics

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:26.880
that Chris Pfund was
talking about as well.

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:28.530
But then even as I'm
listening to you talk,

00:16:28.530 --> 00:16:30.780
I'm also just thinking that
some people may be hearing this

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:33.570
and thinking, "Well, I don't have a space

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:35.700
to actually slow down and take that time."

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:38.010
Or even when we were talking
about the burnout conversation

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:40.755
as well, one of the things
that she brought up was just,

00:16:40.755 --> 00:16:43.140
when people are mentoring
so many different students

00:16:43.140 --> 00:16:44.760
or training others,

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:47.220
feeling the guilt or feeling
like there isn't enough room

00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:49.550
to actually slow down
to get to that place.

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:51.120
So what would you say
to someone like that,

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:54.333
who is feeling that tension
in a very acute kind of way?

00:16:55.375 --> 00:16:56.580
- And I realize I have a certain privilege

00:16:56.580 --> 00:17:01.140
because I'm lead guy, so to
speak, in my organization,

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:02.730
so I really set by schedule.

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:05.130
But I would say to that person
in a very empathetic way

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:08.490
to say, there are things that
can contribute to recharging.

00:17:08.490 --> 00:17:10.260
For example, getting away,

00:17:10.260 --> 00:17:13.650
getting away could be,
it's changing space.

00:17:13.650 --> 00:17:16.710
That means, for example,
beauty recharges the heart.

00:17:16.710 --> 00:17:20.140
It's going into places
where you feel energized.

00:17:20.140 --> 00:17:23.970
Whether it's going to a museum,

00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:26.400
whether it's going to even the mall,

00:17:26.400 --> 00:17:28.980
and you don't have to shop and lose money

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:30.330
in order to get recharged.

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:33.570
But it's beauty that recharges you.

00:17:33.570 --> 00:17:36.431
Not only getting away,
but getting together,

00:17:36.431 --> 00:17:39.600
the social aspect, friends that you know,

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:42.360
it's having conversations, and
it doesn't have to be heady,

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:44.970
technical conversations. It's
just, "How are you doing?"

00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:47.343
It's just talking.

00:17:48.630 --> 00:17:50.010
There's some people that
I'm very close with,

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:51.900
when we get together and talk,

00:17:51.900 --> 00:17:55.230
it's as if everybody else
in the room is invisible,

00:17:55.230 --> 00:17:58.620
because we're so absorbed
in the conversation.

00:17:58.620 --> 00:18:00.030
And the conversation, again,

00:18:00.030 --> 00:18:03.390
may not be technical or heady,
but it's just the company.

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:06.397
So I would encourage individuals that say,

00:18:06.397 --> 00:18:10.110
"Look, I can't take time off."

00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:12.180
But you can take respites.

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:14.970
You can take couple hours break.

00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:16.230
You can do certain things.

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:17.820
So I would say you get away,

00:18:17.820 --> 00:18:20.906
you get together and you get alone.

00:18:20.906 --> 00:18:24.780
The place of being alone
is a very important place

00:18:24.780 --> 00:18:28.320
because it recharges you.

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:31.260
When you're able to think,
process your feelings,

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:34.297
get in touch with yourself,
ask yourself questions like,

00:18:34.297 --> 00:18:36.990
"Who am I? Who am I becoming?

00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:38.340
Who do I want to be?"

00:18:38.340 --> 00:18:41.460
And when you ask those kinds
of pensive reflective questions

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:45.120
in this quiet space, it's amazing.

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:45.953
- Hmm. Yeah.

00:18:45.953 --> 00:18:46.786
That's really well said.

00:18:46.786 --> 00:18:49.320
And I am sure that listeners
will be able to take that in

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:50.340
and enjoy that as well.

00:18:50.340 --> 00:18:52.260
And in a sense, I don't know
if this is the case for you,

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:54.900
but it made me think in a
sense about the juxtaposition

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:56.340
to what you talked about earlier,

00:18:56.340 --> 00:18:58.560
just going, going, going, going.

00:18:58.560 --> 00:19:00.660
I know for me, if I'm in
that mode, talking to others,

00:19:00.660 --> 00:19:04.110
there's often not time
to reflect and to pause.

00:19:04.110 --> 00:19:04.943
And sometimes,

00:19:04.943 --> 00:19:07.500
I mean, I have had the experience
with my students as well,

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:09.360
that place feels productive to them,

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:11.490
but it actually becomes very inefficient

00:19:11.490 --> 00:19:13.290
because they're going so hard,

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:14.820
they don't actually think through things

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:15.653
as well as they could.

00:19:15.653 --> 00:19:18.330
And the pausing actually
creates more space

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:20.220
and increases the productivity

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:22.590
of whatever work they may
be involved in as well.

00:19:22.590 --> 00:19:24.900
So I think that's
really, really important.

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:26.190
- Absolutely. In fact,

00:19:26.190 --> 00:19:29.010
Henry Newman, the gifted
author and theologian,

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:33.894
said that "A person without a
quiet space, a lonely place,

00:19:33.894 --> 00:19:36.120
it's dangerous."

00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:37.535
So we all need to have

00:19:37.535 --> 00:19:40.563
those quiet moments where we reflect.

00:19:40.563 --> 00:19:42.600
And, even if it, again,

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:46.380
is half an hour over the course of a day.

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:48.480
You just need to be able to sit.

00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:51.750
You can have quiet music
or sometimes no music,

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:53.730
and just sit there and just enjoy

00:19:53.730 --> 00:19:56.970
the sacredness of the quiet space.

00:19:56.970 --> 00:19:58.320
It recharges you.

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:02.543
In fact, a sign of burnout is
when there are more mistakes.

00:20:02.543 --> 00:20:04.920
And because you're busy all the time,

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:07.320
so you're not as thoughtful,
not as reflective.

00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:09.510
Another sign is a lack of creativity.

00:20:09.510 --> 00:20:12.600
So it's almost like the
soul has a dashboard.

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:14.700
And when you're going through burnout,

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:18.480
some things increases,
other things decrease.

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:19.350
Those are signs,

00:20:19.350 --> 00:20:20.640
it's almost like when you're looking

00:20:20.640 --> 00:20:22.620
at the dashboard of your car,

00:20:22.620 --> 00:20:25.740
if you're driving and
the things that decrease,

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:28.230
you see the fuel level decreases,

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:30.540
the heat increases of the engine.

00:20:30.540 --> 00:20:32.430
And if you're not careful,

00:20:32.430 --> 00:20:34.500
when we're not looking at
the dashboard of our souls

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:35.820
and being in touch with ourselves,

00:20:35.820 --> 00:20:37.980
we don't know we're at
the verge of burnout.

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:40.620
And so when you find someone
just go off the rails,

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:43.220
you think it just happened?
No, it didn't just happen.

00:20:43.220 --> 00:20:46.260
It was over a period of time,

00:20:46.260 --> 00:20:47.700
and they just weren't monitoring

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:49.470
the dashboard of their
souls, looking at the things

00:20:49.470 --> 00:20:50.730
that were increasing

00:20:50.730 --> 00:20:52.680
and the things that are decreasing.

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:57.543
And so some of the things
that increase is cynicism.

00:20:58.440 --> 00:21:00.030
When someone's at the verge of burnout,

00:21:00.030 --> 00:21:04.140
they're more sarcastic, they're jaded,

00:21:04.140 --> 00:21:06.840
they have this sense of humanus biting.

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:11.490
And it may sound witty and
clever, but it's dangerous.

00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:14.520
And what's even more dangerous
is that oftentimes the cynic

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:19.520
is accurate, but the
cynicism is very destructive.

00:21:19.590 --> 00:21:21.480
And so it's a sign though,

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:23.280
the increased cynicism is a sign

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:25.500
that you're on the verge of burnout.

00:21:25.500 --> 00:21:27.120
And so I want to,

00:21:27.120 --> 00:21:29.280
the listeners to be very cautious of that.

00:21:29.280 --> 00:21:32.353
It's not being witty per se.

00:21:32.353 --> 00:21:35.580
It's showed me that you're empty,

00:21:35.580 --> 00:21:38.490
you're getting empty,
you're becoming empty.

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:39.840
- Yeah. That's really important.

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:41.700
And I'm glad you're
bringing that up as well.

00:21:41.700 --> 00:21:43.560
Would you say that people just,

00:21:43.560 --> 00:21:46.650
making maybe more of a
societal conversation

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:47.483
and perspective,

00:21:47.483 --> 00:21:49.530
are we becoming more aware of that

00:21:49.530 --> 00:21:50.640
and paying more attention to it?

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:53.340
Because I can see different
ways that that cynicism

00:21:53.340 --> 00:21:55.650
may be interpreted or
even rewarded per se,

00:21:55.650 --> 00:21:57.690
because people may still be able to be,

00:21:57.690 --> 00:21:59.310
quote unquote productive or effective,

00:21:59.310 --> 00:22:01.050
even if they are getting
to that place of burnout.

00:22:01.050 --> 00:22:04.380
So what has been your
observation in that sense?

00:22:04.380 --> 00:22:05.213
- In a technical sense,

00:22:05.213 --> 00:22:08.190
I think that individuals
who know what to look for,

00:22:08.190 --> 00:22:11.040
it's almost like a person
who's experienced as a parent

00:22:11.040 --> 00:22:14.610
understands when they
see someone with a child,

00:22:14.610 --> 00:22:16.380
a young child, and they're
just letting them do,

00:22:16.380 --> 00:22:17.730
they're letting them run wild.

00:22:17.730 --> 00:22:20.160
They don't understand what's
gonna happen 20 years from now.

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:24.150
And so an experienced person
sees it and understands it,

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:26.760
an inexperienced person doesn't.

00:22:26.760 --> 00:22:29.880
A careful person who cares
about the individual will bring

00:22:29.880 --> 00:22:32.370
that out to them in a
respectful, honoring way.

00:22:32.370 --> 00:22:34.260
Someone that doesn't really doesn't care,

00:22:34.260 --> 00:22:35.760
they just want to use them,

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:38.581
they'll work them and almost
like drive them to the ground,

00:22:38.581 --> 00:22:40.860
give my stuff and then move on,

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:42.710
and they don't care about the person.

00:22:44.023 --> 00:22:47.130
I know that the Bill and
Melinda Gates Foundation

00:22:47.130 --> 00:22:50.520
did a study in 2020 where they engaged

00:22:50.520 --> 00:22:53.250
the Queensland University,

00:22:53.250 --> 00:22:55.278
and they did a study to try to find out

00:22:55.278 --> 00:23:00.278
the impact of COVID in
regards to the burnout,

00:23:02.610 --> 00:23:05.040
the mental issues, the mental health.

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:07.830
And they saw that there's a 28% increase

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:12.830
in these social disorders
as a result of the pandemic,

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:16.230
the stressors, depressive
disorder, anxiety disorder.

00:23:16.230 --> 00:23:20.490
So depressive disorders
shot up globally 28%,

00:23:20.490 --> 00:23:24.690
anxiety disorder increased by 26% globally

00:23:24.690 --> 00:23:26.430
just because of the pandemic.

00:23:26.430 --> 00:23:29.670
And not that we even changed behaviors,

00:23:29.670 --> 00:23:32.970
just the stressors of
living in our society.

00:23:32.970 --> 00:23:36.330
And so I would say that
particularly people

00:23:36.330 --> 00:23:38.803
that are in leadership roles
have to deal with individuals

00:23:38.803 --> 00:23:40.417
all the time,

00:23:40.417 --> 00:23:43.290
they have to think about
the mental health piece

00:23:43.290 --> 00:23:46.921
and give people spaces to recharge.

00:23:46.921 --> 00:23:49.830
It's so important.

00:23:49.830 --> 00:23:50.670
- Yeah, yeah.

00:23:50.670 --> 00:23:51.960
A hundred percent agree.

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:54.690
And I'd be curious if there
are any anecdotes or stories

00:23:54.690 --> 00:23:56.940
or perspectives you can share on just

00:23:56.940 --> 00:23:58.140
if there are ways that you've actually

00:23:58.140 --> 00:23:59.850
just tried to facilitate that?

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:00.683
'Cause again,

00:24:00.683 --> 00:24:03.090
me being outside of being
in a pastoral ministry role,

00:24:03.090 --> 00:24:05.460
but just seeing the conversations shift,

00:24:05.460 --> 00:24:07.320
especially as a mental health advocate,

00:24:07.320 --> 00:24:09.150
it seems that in the past,

00:24:09.150 --> 00:24:11.220
a lot of that has been pushed to the side

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:12.330
or not acknowledged.

00:24:12.330 --> 00:24:14.460
In the roles when people
are pastors and ministers,

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:17.190
and the expectation just to
continue to meet people's needs

00:24:17.190 --> 00:24:19.440
and meet people's needs
without necessarily being

00:24:19.440 --> 00:24:23.250
as attentive to their own
mental health needs or burnout.

00:24:23.250 --> 00:24:24.870
And so, what has been your experience

00:24:24.870 --> 00:24:26.370
in terms of receptivity,

00:24:26.370 --> 00:24:28.920
and how have you tried to
facilitate that as well?

00:24:28.920 --> 00:24:32.880
- I think having conversations
like this in a very frank,

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:36.150
direct, honest way has been very helpful.

00:24:36.150 --> 00:24:37.950
And so I've made it a very public issue,

00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:41.520
I've spoken on it tons of
times, not only to my staff.

00:24:41.520 --> 00:24:43.824
I have a staff about a hundred people.

00:24:43.824 --> 00:24:47.430
And my leadership team
is about 500 leaders.

00:24:47.430 --> 00:24:51.870
And so I've had to then work
with these men and women

00:24:51.870 --> 00:24:54.540
at different stages in
life and their careers

00:24:54.540 --> 00:24:56.227
and things like that in
order to be able to say,

00:24:56.227 --> 00:24:57.930
"Let's put certain things in place."

00:24:57.930 --> 00:25:00.810
And so we'll put specific things in place,

00:25:00.810 --> 00:25:03.600
such as these sacred
rhythms that I talk about

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:06.270
for those who are part
of our teaching teams.

00:25:06.270 --> 00:25:09.390
We have the concept of the sabbatical

00:25:09.390 --> 00:25:12.210
after six years of ministry,

00:25:12.210 --> 00:25:16.110
they take three months
off and they recharge.

00:25:16.110 --> 00:25:17.490
We have also,

00:25:17.490 --> 00:25:20.430
there are times where
we'll put their names

00:25:20.430 --> 00:25:21.263
on the schedule.

00:25:21.263 --> 00:25:22.950
It says, like for me, no David Ireland,

00:25:22.950 --> 00:25:25.830
so no DI weekend, that
means I'm off the grid.

00:25:25.830 --> 00:25:28.740
No one's gonna be able to get ahold of me.

00:25:28.740 --> 00:25:29.940
And even for the staff,

00:25:29.940 --> 00:25:31.740
there are times that they work from home.

00:25:31.740 --> 00:25:33.480
There are times that they work,

00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:35.070
they're physically there in the office,

00:25:35.070 --> 00:25:38.040
and we give them an opportunity
to say, "Give us input.

00:25:38.040 --> 00:25:39.420
How are you doing?"

00:25:39.420 --> 00:25:42.210
We created the chaplaincy program,

00:25:42.210 --> 00:25:43.897
so that even for the staff to say,

00:25:43.897 --> 00:25:46.763
"Okay, you go and meet with the chaplains

00:25:46.763 --> 00:25:49.140
that we have on staff,

00:25:49.140 --> 00:25:51.033
the mental health professionals,

00:25:51.033 --> 00:25:53.880
unpack whatever needs to be unpacked,

00:25:53.880 --> 00:25:55.830
we want you to be mentally healthy.

00:25:55.830 --> 00:25:57.153
And so we have that,

00:25:58.260 --> 00:26:01.027
and then the check-ins
periodically just to say,

00:26:01.027 --> 00:26:04.170
"How are you doing? What's going on?"

00:26:04.170 --> 00:26:08.327
Because oftentimes you
don't see the signs,

00:26:08.327 --> 00:26:10.440
'cause if one of the
signs is productivity,

00:26:10.440 --> 00:26:12.450
but there are other signs,

00:26:12.450 --> 00:26:14.160
the loss of empathy,

00:26:14.160 --> 00:26:16.380
the issue of compassion fatigue,

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:19.260
where I'm tired of caring,
I don't care anymore,

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:20.580
it's too exhausting.

00:26:20.580 --> 00:26:23.910
And particularly when you're
in a caring profession,

00:26:23.910 --> 00:26:26.760
when you're tired of
caring, there's a problem.

00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:29.113
- Yeah. Exactly.

00:26:29.113 --> 00:26:31.770
That's very encouraging to hear in the way

00:26:31.770 --> 00:26:32.603
that you've described it,

00:26:32.603 --> 00:26:35.730
that you've built it
into the culture as well.

00:26:35.730 --> 00:26:38.010
And it seems like also leading by example,

00:26:38.010 --> 00:26:38.850
even in your own,

00:26:38.850 --> 00:26:40.740
'cause you talked about those
rhythms in your own life

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:42.930
and making sure that that's
baked in for everyone else.

00:26:42.930 --> 00:26:45.990
Are there ways that you've
seen that perpetuate

00:26:45.990 --> 00:26:48.060
amongst your staff and
amongst your community

00:26:48.060 --> 00:26:50.730
just as you've kind of
tried to bake it in?

00:26:50.730 --> 00:26:51.563
- Yeah,

00:26:51.563 --> 00:26:54.948
what I've seen is that they've
accepted the limitations,

00:26:54.948 --> 00:26:57.360
and limitations in the sense

00:26:57.360 --> 00:27:01.260
that not everyone has the same capacity.

00:27:01.260 --> 00:27:03.210
There's some people
that can juggle 15 balls

00:27:03.210 --> 00:27:04.043
at the same time,

00:27:04.043 --> 00:27:05.670
others that can only juggle two,

00:27:05.670 --> 00:27:07.320
and one's not better than the other.

00:27:07.320 --> 00:27:08.400
They're just different,

00:27:08.400 --> 00:27:10.200
and so we have to be aware of that.

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:11.640
The same way children,

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:14.850
I'm not gonna expect a six
year old to be able to speak

00:27:14.850 --> 00:27:16.704
as eloquently as a 15 year old,,

00:27:16.704 --> 00:27:21.704
or the six year old to be as
patient as a college student.

00:27:23.370 --> 00:27:25.020
I just won't expect that.

00:27:25.020 --> 00:27:26.790
And so I'm not gonna
place pressures on them.

00:27:26.790 --> 00:27:29.250
So, to answer your question,

00:27:29.250 --> 00:27:31.980
people have accepted their limitations,

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:33.930
accepted their capacity,

00:27:33.930 --> 00:27:37.410
and accepted the frequency
of what they need.

00:27:37.410 --> 00:27:38.580
I remember several years ago,

00:27:38.580 --> 00:27:42.420
my wife and I were hosting a
marriage seminar for a group.

00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:46.260
And at one point in the seminar,

00:27:46.260 --> 00:27:48.810
my wife spoke to all the
husbands in a separate room.

00:27:48.810 --> 00:27:50.160
I spoke to all the wives,

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:51.710
and so that's always dangerous.

00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:56.629
But after the presentation,

00:27:56.629 --> 00:27:58.800
some of the women asked questions.

00:27:58.800 --> 00:28:00.360
One woman was very vulnerable.

00:28:00.360 --> 00:28:02.553
She said, "My husband and I,

00:28:03.503 --> 00:28:04.440
we have two special needs kids

00:28:04.440 --> 00:28:08.783
and I find myself yelling
at them so frequently,

00:28:08.783 --> 00:28:10.947
how do I deal with that?"

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:13.140
And my answer to her,

00:28:13.140 --> 00:28:16.470
it seemed as if I wasn't even
listening to her question.

00:28:16.470 --> 00:28:19.830
I asked, because my answer
raised another question.

00:28:19.830 --> 00:28:23.127
I said to her, "How often
do you take vacation?"

00:28:24.570 --> 00:28:26.130
And then she paused,

00:28:26.130 --> 00:28:29.970
because there'd not been
a frequency of vacation.

00:28:29.970 --> 00:28:30.907
And I said,

00:28:30.907 --> 00:28:33.300
"Let's not even talk about a vacation

00:28:33.300 --> 00:28:35.610
where you go away for a couple of weeks.

00:28:35.610 --> 00:28:37.560
How often do you take a respite

00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:40.140
from providing the care to your children?"

00:28:40.140 --> 00:28:41.250
And I said to her,

00:28:41.250 --> 00:28:45.708
"You're not a bad mother,
you're just a tired mother.

00:28:45.708 --> 00:28:48.270
And what you need is to build then

00:28:48.270 --> 00:28:50.130
these sacred rhythms in your life.

00:28:50.130 --> 00:28:52.020
And you may need more of that

00:28:52.020 --> 00:28:54.780
because your children have special needs."

00:28:54.780 --> 00:28:57.780
And so it requires a more frequent respite

00:28:57.780 --> 00:28:59.340
than someone who may not be caring

00:28:59.340 --> 00:29:00.570
for special needs children.

00:29:00.570 --> 00:29:02.130
And I think it's giving people permission

00:29:02.130 --> 00:29:04.200
to say, "You are a human being."

00:29:04.200 --> 00:29:08.790
And don't label yourself
in a moralized way

00:29:08.790 --> 00:29:11.700
when the problem is not a moral problem,

00:29:11.700 --> 00:29:13.699
the problem is a behavioral one,

00:29:13.699 --> 00:29:16.410
in the sense that you just
need respite in your life.

00:29:16.410 --> 00:29:20.190
And sometimes for us it's
philosophical and even cultural.

00:29:20.190 --> 00:29:21.487
If you come from a culture that says,

00:29:21.487 --> 00:29:25.290
"Look, if you take a break,
you're lazy and you're bad."

00:29:25.290 --> 00:29:29.572
Then we need to reframe
what the problem really is.

00:29:29.572 --> 00:29:31.800
- Yeah. That's so powerful, so well said.

00:29:31.800 --> 00:29:34.410
It actually relates
directly to even a question

00:29:34.410 --> 00:29:36.600
that Dr. Pfund and I were
talking through yesterday

00:29:36.600 --> 00:29:40.530
in that conversation, in terms
of mentoring and educating,

00:29:40.530 --> 00:29:42.000
but also in ministry,

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:45.270
the challenge of saying
no to someone in need.

00:29:45.270 --> 00:29:46.440
So in that example you gave,

00:29:46.440 --> 00:29:48.030
even someone with special needs children,

00:29:48.030 --> 00:29:49.710
and it seemed like part of it was,

00:29:49.710 --> 00:29:52.170
I can't say no to them because
I have to be there for them

00:29:52.170 --> 00:29:53.370
and can keep to continue.

00:29:53.370 --> 00:29:56.250
So one thing that Chris
Pfund raised as well was just

00:29:56.250 --> 00:29:57.750
that tension was something
that she had been

00:29:57.750 --> 00:29:58.890
thinking through and wrestling

00:29:58.890 --> 00:30:01.049
even as we were preparing for this episode

00:30:01.049 --> 00:30:05.280
with her own Christian
life experience as well,

00:30:05.280 --> 00:30:08.280
and the concept of selfless
love and those types of things.

00:30:08.280 --> 00:30:12.450
And how do we navigate in
that space actually saying no

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:13.500
to someone that's in need,

00:30:13.500 --> 00:30:15.090
versus quote unquote self-care,

00:30:15.090 --> 00:30:17.100
when that doesn't always fit the narrative

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:21.061
that we are taught and talk about,

00:30:21.061 --> 00:30:22.223
so I think the way you described
that is really helpful.

00:30:22.223 --> 00:30:23.490
I dunno if there's anything else

00:30:23.490 --> 00:30:25.507
that you wanna kinda add to that question.

00:30:25.507 --> 00:30:26.566
- And I think I would add to it

00:30:26.566 --> 00:30:30.090
that it's not wrong for me
to provide care for myself.

00:30:30.090 --> 00:30:32.755
It's almost like the dialogue that we hear

00:30:32.755 --> 00:30:36.547
when we get on an airplane, they tell us,

00:30:36.547 --> 00:30:39.360
"Look, first put your own mask on

00:30:39.360 --> 00:30:41.340
before you help that person,

00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:44.190
whether you're a child or an aged parent

00:30:44.190 --> 00:30:46.050
to put on their mask."

00:30:46.050 --> 00:30:51.050
And it seems almost contrary to logic,

00:30:51.120 --> 00:30:52.650
but it is logical.

00:30:52.650 --> 00:30:54.870
And so when I care for myself,

00:30:54.870 --> 00:30:59.130
and it's not about me being egotistical

00:30:59.130 --> 00:31:02.010
or feeling that I'm more
valuable than the other,

00:31:02.010 --> 00:31:04.297
it's about, I can't be
of any value to anyone

00:31:04.297 --> 00:31:09.297
if I'm falling apart or if I'm
so broken or if I'm so needy.

00:31:09.466 --> 00:31:11.763
It doesn't work that way.

00:31:13.590 --> 00:31:16.170
And so really it's about planning.

00:31:16.170 --> 00:31:19.530
And so even to that mom with
the special needs children,

00:31:19.530 --> 00:31:21.157
it's about planning where you can say,

00:31:21.157 --> 00:31:23.880
"Look, I'm gonna hire a babysitter.

00:31:23.880 --> 00:31:27.030
I'm going to get my
parents to come and sit

00:31:27.030 --> 00:31:29.730
for a Friday evening so my husband and I

00:31:29.730 --> 00:31:31.288
can go out to the movies."

00:31:31.288 --> 00:31:34.769
It's about putting some structure
in place that's practical

00:31:34.769 --> 00:31:38.100
to have these respite
so that you can function

00:31:38.100 --> 00:31:40.140
in an emotionally
healthy way, it's needed.

00:31:40.140 --> 00:31:42.480
- Yeah, I highly agree.

00:31:42.480 --> 00:31:43.950
Just to push the envelope
a little bit further,

00:31:43.950 --> 00:31:44.820
'cause I know again,

00:31:44.820 --> 00:31:46.200
this is just something that I know people

00:31:46.200 --> 00:31:47.033
are wrestling with,

00:31:47.033 --> 00:31:49.530
so even with all of those good practices,

00:31:49.530 --> 00:31:52.036
there's still sometimes
that feeling of guilt,

00:31:52.036 --> 00:31:54.840
that I'm still not doing what I should

00:31:54.840 --> 00:31:58.710
or perfectionism, or even
a mixture of the two.

00:31:58.710 --> 00:32:00.240
So how have you kind of helped people,

00:32:00.240 --> 00:32:01.590
and maybe it's just practice in time,

00:32:01.590 --> 00:32:04.890
but really kind of move from
that either for themselves

00:32:04.890 --> 00:32:07.020
or I would say even the stigma that comes.

00:32:07.020 --> 00:32:10.020
'Cause even as we've talked
about people in leadership,

00:32:10.020 --> 00:32:12.810
pastors and ministers having
more space for themselves,

00:32:12.810 --> 00:32:14.700
it seems that when there is a breakdown,

00:32:14.700 --> 00:32:16.770
it's still so easy for many of us to get

00:32:16.770 --> 00:32:18.300
to that place of judgment.

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:20.520
Like, "Oh, how could they falter?

00:32:20.520 --> 00:32:21.990
They're supposed to be the model.

00:32:21.990 --> 00:32:23.400
We're looking up to them.

00:32:23.400 --> 00:32:24.660
If they're struggling,

00:32:24.660 --> 00:32:26.580
then how are the rest of
us supposed to be able

00:32:26.580 --> 00:32:29.640
to make it through a more
quote unquote following them?

00:32:29.640 --> 00:32:32.730
- And I'll say that there
are times when I take breaks,

00:32:32.730 --> 00:32:35.617
I still at times struggle with
a little bit of guilt saying,

00:32:35.617 --> 00:32:36.560
"Look, I should be doing this

00:32:36.560 --> 00:32:38.430
or I should be busy doing that,

00:32:38.430 --> 00:32:41.100
or that thing is hanging over my head."

00:32:41.100 --> 00:32:46.100
And, so I've never completely
overcome the idea of that.

00:32:47.219 --> 00:32:50.550
And I think in some regards,
it's always gonna be there,

00:32:50.550 --> 00:32:52.967
because it's just the human experience.

00:32:52.967 --> 00:32:54.900
And it's okay.

00:32:54.900 --> 00:32:56.220
That's the point I'm bringing out,

00:32:56.220 --> 00:32:58.027
I think the tension of saying,

00:32:58.027 --> 00:33:02.520
"The things I can do, but
yet I need time to recharge."

00:33:02.520 --> 00:33:03.810
That tension's going to be there.

00:33:03.810 --> 00:33:05.850
And I think the tension's
healthy because it's saying

00:33:05.850 --> 00:33:08.970
in essence that I want to be responsible

00:33:08.970 --> 00:33:12.720
and I wanna maintain a
lifestyle of being responsible

00:33:12.720 --> 00:33:17.250
in all of my areas of
oversight, so to speak.

00:33:17.250 --> 00:33:18.540
But in the same breath,

00:33:18.540 --> 00:33:20.340
I'm going to make sure I take care of me,

00:33:20.340 --> 00:33:22.950
and I think that's something
that's so important.

00:33:22.950 --> 00:33:25.950
I can't just soldier on
and think that it's okay,

00:33:25.950 --> 00:33:27.330
it doesn't work that way.

00:33:27.330 --> 00:33:28.230
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:33:28.230 --> 00:33:29.699
Well I think that's so important.

00:33:29.699 --> 00:33:32.160
We can't pretend that
the guilt's not there,

00:33:32.160 --> 00:33:33.840
and to still experience it,

00:33:33.840 --> 00:33:35.700
but to know that it's still
a process that we're moving

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:37.620
through as well. I think
it's really helpful

00:33:37.620 --> 00:33:38.790
- It's a learned experience.

00:33:38.790 --> 00:33:42.630
Even Jesus in sacred
scripture tells his disciples

00:33:42.630 --> 00:33:46.117
after a busy stint of
ministry, he says to them,

00:33:46.117 --> 00:33:48.570
"Come with me by
yourselves to a quiet place

00:33:48.570 --> 00:33:50.400
and get some rest."

00:33:50.400 --> 00:33:51.337
And so he is saying to them,

00:33:51.337 --> 00:33:54.810
"Look, you guys don't just soldier on

00:33:54.810 --> 00:33:57.540
even though what you're
doing is so critical."

00:33:57.540 --> 00:34:00.450
And he himself as that critical leader,

00:34:00.450 --> 00:34:01.283
he's saying, "Look,

00:34:01.283 --> 00:34:03.600
I need to recharge, 'cause
if I don't recharge,

00:34:03.600 --> 00:34:05.250
I'm not going to be effective

00:34:05.250 --> 00:34:07.080
or I'm not gonna continue
being effective."

00:34:07.080 --> 00:34:09.600
And he was teaching them
that discipline of really

00:34:09.600 --> 00:34:11.160
safeguarding their mental health.

00:34:11.160 --> 00:34:12.030
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:34:12.030 --> 00:34:13.830
I think that's really well said.

00:34:13.830 --> 00:34:15.780
So as we've been talking,
just to pivot a little bit,

00:34:15.780 --> 00:34:19.890
I'm also wondering a little
bit about legacy in a sense,

00:34:19.890 --> 00:34:22.620
but then also thinking
about the next generation.

00:34:22.620 --> 00:34:23.970
And one reason that came up,

00:34:23.970 --> 00:34:26.010
as we're talking about the
increased rates of depression,

00:34:26.010 --> 00:34:27.540
increased rates of anxiety,

00:34:27.540 --> 00:34:29.668
obviously that's been
a topic of conversation

00:34:29.668 --> 00:34:31.530
for our youth as well.

00:34:31.530 --> 00:34:34.830
So I'm curious if that's something
that you've thought about

00:34:34.830 --> 00:34:36.810
that's come into play
as you've talked about

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:38.340
a lot of these principles or even burnout

00:34:38.340 --> 00:34:40.170
and if that is the case,

00:34:40.170 --> 00:34:42.210
how you've tried to not translate,

00:34:42.210 --> 00:34:45.574
but try to impart that on
the next generation as well?

00:34:45.574 --> 00:34:47.310
- I'm involved in succession planning.

00:34:47.310 --> 00:34:51.528
I do consulting in that regard
as well with senior leaders.

00:34:51.528 --> 00:34:54.180
But to your point,

00:34:54.180 --> 00:34:57.870
I think it's really helping
to create a cultural awareness

00:34:57.870 --> 00:35:01.313
and a personal philosophy
of leadership that says,

00:35:01.313 --> 00:35:06.313
establish and safeguard sacred rhythms.

00:35:07.650 --> 00:35:10.320
So you're teaching them,
they're doing it anyway,

00:35:10.320 --> 00:35:12.480
but they're doing it under the principal

00:35:12.480 --> 00:35:15.870
that may be very quote unquote unhealthy.

00:35:15.870 --> 00:35:17.760
And so I encourage parents, I said, "Look,

00:35:17.760 --> 00:35:20.654
when your children have
been in school all day,

00:35:20.654 --> 00:35:22.767
and when they come home,
don't just simply say,

00:35:22.767 --> 00:35:24.600
'Go do your homework.'

00:35:24.600 --> 00:35:27.360
Let 'em take a break, let 'em recharge."

00:35:27.360 --> 00:35:29.280
And I think that for some parents,

00:35:29.280 --> 00:35:32.340
it took a long time to wrap
their minds around that

00:35:32.340 --> 00:35:35.407
because they came from a
family of origin that says,

00:35:35.407 --> 00:35:38.700
"Go do your homework
and then you can relax."

00:35:38.700 --> 00:35:40.710
And I said, "Flip the
switch, let them relax,

00:35:40.710 --> 00:35:43.080
put some times constraints on it,

00:35:43.080 --> 00:35:45.000
and then have them do their homework."

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:47.850
So this way, they feel emotionally whole,

00:35:47.850 --> 00:35:50.190
because I think that if
we're creating children

00:35:50.190 --> 00:35:53.460
and even the next generation
that are emotionally unhealthy,

00:35:53.460 --> 00:35:54.750
they won't have longevity.

00:35:54.750 --> 00:35:57.330
And I think the proof
of a good mental health

00:35:57.330 --> 00:36:00.090
is that there's sustainability,
there's longevity.

00:36:00.090 --> 00:36:03.750
You're not doing something
quick and then after five years,

00:36:03.750 --> 00:36:05.400
you're burnt out.

00:36:05.400 --> 00:36:07.710
There must be some
sustainability to what you do.

00:36:07.710 --> 00:36:08.543
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:36:08.543 --> 00:36:10.737
I think that's really,
really well said as well.

00:36:10.737 --> 00:36:13.110
And even though this terms
of this burnout conversation,

00:36:13.110 --> 00:36:15.870
that conversation that
you feel like is helpful,

00:36:15.870 --> 00:36:18.300
that you've been able to have
with young people as well,

00:36:18.300 --> 00:36:20.730
and if are the signs
different or is it similar

00:36:20.730 --> 00:36:21.720
to what you mentioned before,

00:36:21.720 --> 00:36:25.290
even in terms of some of that
cynicism and lack of empathy?

00:36:25.290 --> 00:36:29.850
- I think both the title, if
you use the title burnout,

00:36:29.850 --> 00:36:31.053
it drives people away.

00:36:33.420 --> 00:36:36.390
But if I use the word sustainability,

00:36:36.390 --> 00:36:41.390
if I have to wordsmith it in
order to make it attractional,

00:36:41.550 --> 00:36:44.190
because if I say for example,
how to avoid burnout,

00:36:44.190 --> 00:36:46.560
no one's coming to that seminar.

00:36:46.560 --> 00:36:49.447
But if I say, how to be
effective for a long time,

00:36:49.447 --> 00:36:50.577
"Okay, I'm going."

00:36:51.952 --> 00:36:55.650
So it's almost reframing the statement

00:36:55.650 --> 00:36:58.350
so it becomes quote unquote sexy

00:36:58.350 --> 00:37:01.230
and attractive to leaders,
both young and old.

00:37:01.230 --> 00:37:02.730
And that's what I've found to be the case.

00:37:02.730 --> 00:37:04.598
So I've had to change titles,

00:37:04.598 --> 00:37:07.777
having been the recipient of saying,

00:37:07.777 --> 00:37:09.300
"I'm not going to that workshop,

00:37:09.300 --> 00:37:10.910
I'm going to the one."

00:37:10.910 --> 00:37:14.790
- Yeah. Making it palatable
and attractive to people.

00:37:14.790 --> 00:37:15.840
I mean the packaging.

00:37:17.580 --> 00:37:20.670
Well I know we already had Chris Pfund on,

00:37:20.670 --> 00:37:22.560
but I'm curious if there are any,

00:37:22.560 --> 00:37:23.820
well I'll figure out a way to do this,

00:37:23.820 --> 00:37:25.260
but if there was
anything, if she was here,

00:37:25.260 --> 00:37:27.990
if there's any questions
you would have posed to her

00:37:27.990 --> 00:37:29.550
in terms of the mentorship piece,

00:37:29.550 --> 00:37:31.170
talking specifically in academic spaces

00:37:31.170 --> 00:37:33.824
or training or just any
thoughts around that?

00:37:33.824 --> 00:37:36.930
- I'm mentoring as well.

00:37:36.930 --> 00:37:38.550
I think every space there's mentoring,

00:37:38.550 --> 00:37:40.110
'cause there has to be succession,

00:37:40.110 --> 00:37:42.690
you have to pass on to others
the things you've learned

00:37:42.690 --> 00:37:43.523
over the years.

00:37:43.523 --> 00:37:45.480
And I think I would just commend her

00:37:45.480 --> 00:37:49.057
for the discipline of saying,

00:37:49.057 --> 00:37:52.470
"I have a responsibility
to the next generation.

00:37:52.470 --> 00:37:57.470
They should build on the
things that I've built,

00:37:57.660 --> 00:37:58.493
so to speak.

00:37:58.493 --> 00:38:00.869
And so they stand on my
shoulders so they can go farther

00:38:00.869 --> 00:38:02.820
and be able to do more things."

00:38:02.820 --> 00:38:06.397
And so it does require then
for me to look behind and say,

00:38:06.397 --> 00:38:08.550
"Let's pull them forward."

00:38:08.550 --> 00:38:12.482
I remember, I had this conversation,

00:38:12.482 --> 00:38:17.370
it was me and about maybe 20
or 30 other people I served

00:38:17.370 --> 00:38:20.670
on the executive committee
for the last crusade

00:38:20.670 --> 00:38:22.203
of Dr. Billy Graham's life.

00:38:23.070 --> 00:38:26.850
It was the crusade of
2005 in New York City.

00:38:26.850 --> 00:38:31.493
And I remember Dr. Graham
said something, he said this,

00:38:31.493 --> 00:38:36.493
"I wish I had made friends that
were a lot younger than me,

00:38:37.530 --> 00:38:40.467
because all my friends have died."

00:38:42.311 --> 00:38:43.830
I mean it's one of those,

00:38:43.830 --> 00:38:45.330
sometimes you hear
something, you write it down,

00:38:45.330 --> 00:38:46.650
other times it's written on you.

00:38:46.650 --> 00:38:47.700
That was written on me.

00:38:47.700 --> 00:38:52.350
So to Chris Pfund and to other mentors,

00:38:52.350 --> 00:38:55.620
I would say, being a mentor
means that you're making friends

00:38:55.620 --> 00:38:57.870
with people that are younger, the protege,

00:38:57.870 --> 00:38:59.910
younger in experience, younger in age.

00:38:59.910 --> 00:39:03.630
But this is what one of
the quintessential leaders,

00:39:03.630 --> 00:39:07.687
Dr. Billy Graham was remiss about saying,

00:39:07.687 --> 00:39:10.816
"Man, I wish I would've
spent more time doing that."

00:39:10.816 --> 00:39:15.480
I think it's dangerous when we only

00:39:15.480 --> 00:39:16.890
are concerned about ourselves,

00:39:16.890 --> 00:39:19.140
and our pushing things forward,

00:39:19.140 --> 00:39:20.910
and our level of productivity

00:39:20.910 --> 00:39:23.100
and building quote unquote our kingdoms,

00:39:23.100 --> 00:39:24.240
and our organizations.

00:39:24.240 --> 00:39:27.810
So there must be a turning
around to the next generation

00:39:27.810 --> 00:39:28.643
to say,

00:39:28.643 --> 00:39:30.870
"I have a responsibility
to help you guys fulfill

00:39:30.870 --> 00:39:34.170
your destiny and create legacy for you,

00:39:34.170 --> 00:39:36.270
so let me spend myself helping you."

00:39:36.270 --> 00:39:38.607
So in other words,

00:39:38.607 --> 00:39:42.030
I have to recharge 'cause
I have responsibility

00:39:42.030 --> 00:39:43.500
to the next generation.

00:39:43.500 --> 00:39:45.930
So if I don't maintain
my own mental health,

00:39:45.930 --> 00:39:47.980
then I'm being irresponsible as a leader.

00:39:49.260 --> 00:39:50.190
- Yeah. That's so powerful.

00:39:50.190 --> 00:39:51.930
I mean, even thinking of my own journey,

00:39:51.930 --> 00:39:55.080
as things have moved over
the years that tendency,

00:39:55.080 --> 00:39:58.050
and that pull sometimes to shift,

00:39:58.050 --> 00:39:58.920
to continue to mentor,

00:39:58.920 --> 00:40:01.140
but to shift the circle as it were,

00:40:01.140 --> 00:40:03.240
to peers in order to
learn from everything.

00:40:03.240 --> 00:40:05.790
But that comment that you mentioned,

00:40:05.790 --> 00:40:09.660
it's so powerful of what can
be missed if that goes too far,

00:40:09.660 --> 00:40:10.530
and just the power of that.

00:40:10.530 --> 00:40:11.670
And Chris mentioned that as well,

00:40:11.670 --> 00:40:14.313
just the privilege of being able to mentor

00:40:14.313 --> 00:40:16.770
and impact the next generation.

00:40:16.770 --> 00:40:18.420
So I think that's really something

00:40:18.420 --> 00:40:20.418
that will definitely sit with me as well,

00:40:20.418 --> 00:40:21.892
and I'm sure with our listeners.

00:40:21.892 --> 00:40:22.725
- I appreciate that.

00:40:22.725 --> 00:40:24.683
And it still sits with me also

00:40:24.683 --> 00:40:27.210
'cause I have that responsibility.

00:40:27.210 --> 00:40:28.650
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:40:28.650 --> 00:40:30.630
And then I know I keep bringing
things up that she said

00:40:30.630 --> 00:40:31.800
as well, but just to tie 'em together,

00:40:31.800 --> 00:40:35.640
just the modeling and the
example that can come from

00:40:35.640 --> 00:40:36.473
that as well.

00:40:36.473 --> 00:40:38.250
So one thing that she shared
about one of the studies

00:40:38.250 --> 00:40:40.710
that they had done,
specifically around burnout

00:40:40.710 --> 00:40:43.950
was just asking some of
those who were being mentored

00:40:43.950 --> 00:40:45.600
about their experiences.

00:40:45.600 --> 00:40:49.525
And some of them, this can fit
into pastoral care as well.

00:40:49.525 --> 00:40:51.960
But looking at mentors and
thinking that those individuals

00:40:51.960 --> 00:40:54.240
never dealt with things like burnout

00:40:54.240 --> 00:40:55.800
or some of those mental health challenges.

00:40:55.800 --> 00:40:58.470
And then those individuals
feeling dissuaded

00:40:58.470 --> 00:41:00.600
and not wanting to go
into certain professions

00:41:00.600 --> 00:41:01.597
because they were saying,

00:41:01.597 --> 00:41:04.522
"Well if this person never
struggles with burnout,

00:41:04.522 --> 00:41:06.690
this isn't something I can do,

00:41:06.690 --> 00:41:08.340
so I think I need to
opt and go elsewhere."

00:41:08.340 --> 00:41:10.350
Which is a fallacy in and of itself.

00:41:10.350 --> 00:41:12.090
So I think it speaks
even to all the things

00:41:12.090 --> 00:41:13.560
that you talked about before,

00:41:13.560 --> 00:41:15.960
that what you've baked into
culture and leading by example,

00:41:15.960 --> 00:41:16.920
that transparency,

00:41:16.920 --> 00:41:19.260
even as you talked about
your initial experience,

00:41:19.260 --> 00:41:20.280
I believe it was in Zambia,

00:41:20.280 --> 00:41:21.930
having that transparency involved,

00:41:21.930 --> 00:41:24.660
how powerful that is even
in terms of the impact

00:41:24.660 --> 00:41:26.070
in the next generation.

00:41:26.070 --> 00:41:27.870
- And I think even on the topic of burnout

00:41:27.870 --> 00:41:30.840
is such a sensitive topic
that some people, it's almost,

00:41:30.840 --> 00:41:35.220
I have to take a play out of
the playbook of individuals

00:41:35.220 --> 00:41:36.720
that take an autocratic position,

00:41:36.720 --> 00:41:39.930
saying I have to impose
respite for some people

00:41:39.930 --> 00:41:40.860
because they're gonna keep running

00:41:40.860 --> 00:41:42.150
until the wheels fall off.

00:41:42.150 --> 00:41:45.000
And so I have to then put it
in place of this is mandatory,

00:41:45.000 --> 00:41:46.140
this is necessary.

00:41:46.140 --> 00:41:47.370
You have to do this,

00:41:47.370 --> 00:41:49.170
you can't come to work tomorrow.

00:41:49.170 --> 00:41:50.798
This needs to take place,

00:41:50.798 --> 00:41:53.384
because they're not seeing it,

00:41:53.384 --> 00:41:57.180
or they're not accepting
their human frailty.

00:41:57.180 --> 00:42:00.210
And I think that is a reality
also for some leaders.

00:42:00.210 --> 00:42:03.150
And again, it ties into
that whole cultural piece.

00:42:03.150 --> 00:42:05.418
If they came from a cultural
background that says,

00:42:05.418 --> 00:42:08.100
you don't take time off, that's not right.

00:42:08.100 --> 00:42:08.933
That's wrong.

00:42:08.933 --> 00:42:10.590
And they look at it as a moral issue,

00:42:10.590 --> 00:42:13.171
ethical issue when it's not that.

00:42:13.171 --> 00:42:15.007
It's a lifestyle issue to say,

00:42:15.007 --> 00:42:16.260
"I want sustainability,

00:42:16.260 --> 00:42:19.996
and I must be able to not
only enjoy the work of my hand

00:42:19.996 --> 00:42:22.715
and the work of my mind, but
I must enjoy relationships

00:42:22.715 --> 00:42:26.250
and make sure I enjoy
myself that I'm healthy."

00:42:26.250 --> 00:42:27.328
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:42:27.328 --> 00:42:28.161
And again,

00:42:28.161 --> 00:42:29.820
I just think that speaks
about your effectiveness

00:42:29.820 --> 00:42:31.345
as a leader as well.

00:42:31.345 --> 00:42:33.690
Sometimes more of a delegate mode,

00:42:33.690 --> 00:42:34.980
sometimes more autocratic,

00:42:34.980 --> 00:42:35.813
and in that instance,

00:42:35.813 --> 00:42:37.957
I think the autocratic
is so helpful to say,

00:42:37.957 --> 00:42:39.450
"This is something that you need."

00:42:39.450 --> 00:42:41.960
And to lead by that example
and to put into place.

00:42:41.960 --> 00:42:42.840
In my own experience,

00:42:42.840 --> 00:42:44.520
I've seen people in a sense,

00:42:44.520 --> 00:42:47.017
slowly incorporate that
and then come back and say,

00:42:47.017 --> 00:42:49.080
"Oh, okay, I actually
realized that I do need this".

00:42:49.080 --> 00:42:51.510
And to have the benefits
of that after the fact,

00:42:51.510 --> 00:42:53.400
I think is is huge and tremendous.

00:42:53.400 --> 00:42:54.750
- It's so important.

00:42:54.750 --> 00:42:56.220
It's so important.

00:42:56.220 --> 00:42:58.350
- Yeah. So I did wanna incorporate,

00:42:58.350 --> 00:43:00.570
'cause we had some questions
from our audience as well,

00:43:00.570 --> 00:43:01.403
from our listeners.

00:43:01.403 --> 00:43:03.030
And I think you've actually
touched on this pretty well,

00:43:03.030 --> 00:43:05.760
but I'll just make sure that
I state the question as well.

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:07.152
But people are asking

00:43:07.152 --> 00:43:10.110
about all these aspects of either avoiding

00:43:10.110 --> 00:43:11.940
or kind of navigating through burnout,

00:43:11.940 --> 00:43:13.920
especially when they
feel like more and more

00:43:13.920 --> 00:43:16.110
responsibilities are being added on.

00:43:16.110 --> 00:43:18.270
Kind of that sense that some people have

00:43:18.270 --> 00:43:20.970
that those who are doing good
work then get given more work.

00:43:20.970 --> 00:43:22.680
And I know you've talked
about this before,

00:43:22.680 --> 00:43:24.420
but would you say to that person

00:43:24.420 --> 00:43:25.710
who is either feeling to push

00:43:25.710 --> 00:43:27.420
because they have to, for instance,

00:43:27.420 --> 00:43:30.120
get promoted or move up the line,

00:43:30.120 --> 00:43:32.430
or there's so many tasks in front of them.

00:43:32.430 --> 00:43:33.263
And again,

00:43:33.263 --> 00:43:35.100
if there's anything you want
to add just to that person,

00:43:35.100 --> 00:43:37.680
who's saying, "I don't know
how to incorporate this."

00:43:37.680 --> 00:43:38.513
- And I think that

00:43:38.513 --> 00:43:41.791
what I've seen leaders do
is that they've negotiated

00:43:41.791 --> 00:43:45.810
responsibilities with their
employers or their supervisors.

00:43:45.810 --> 00:43:47.257
In other words, if their supervisor says,

00:43:47.257 --> 00:43:49.860
"This person's very effective,
I need to give them this

00:43:49.860 --> 00:43:51.960
because I know they'll do a good job."

00:43:51.960 --> 00:43:55.080
They then, the employee
then has to be vocal

00:43:55.080 --> 00:43:58.680
in a respectful way and say,
"Here's what I'm doing now.

00:43:58.680 --> 00:44:01.410
If I add this on my plate,
something's going to give.

00:44:01.410 --> 00:44:04.650
So of these, let's say 10
things that I'm carrying,

00:44:04.650 --> 00:44:06.839
what would you like me to
give up in order to take on

00:44:06.839 --> 00:44:08.545
this 11th?"

00:44:08.545 --> 00:44:13.230
And put it back in the leader's plate.

00:44:13.230 --> 00:44:17.370
So the leader begins to think
about priorities and timelines

00:44:17.370 --> 00:44:18.210
and what's reasonable,

00:44:18.210 --> 00:44:20.340
because sometimes they're
not conscious of it

00:44:20.340 --> 00:44:23.400
because they're carrying a
whole big load themselves.

00:44:23.400 --> 00:44:25.950
And I think the negotiation
process is important.

00:44:25.950 --> 00:44:28.320
And even with the advent
of working from home,

00:44:28.320 --> 00:44:31.110
what I've seen some leaders do is the fact

00:44:31.110 --> 00:44:32.292
that they've said, "Look,

00:44:32.292 --> 00:44:35.297
I will not take any
meetings before 8:00 AM

00:44:35.297 --> 00:44:37.920
'cause I wanna take my kid to school

00:44:37.920 --> 00:44:39.780
and nothing's gonna
get in the way of that,

00:44:39.780 --> 00:44:42.330
'cause it's necessary for me to sit,

00:44:42.330 --> 00:44:45.389
to be in the car and have those
conversations with my kids

00:44:45.389 --> 00:44:50.389
and to check in with them,
and they block out things."

00:44:50.873 --> 00:44:52.798
And so I think that the
idea is to negotiate

00:44:52.798 --> 00:44:56.580
with your supervisors these realities,

00:44:56.580 --> 00:45:00.232
and let 'em know that
you're starting to see signs

00:45:00.232 --> 00:45:02.370
of burnout in your own life

00:45:02.370 --> 00:45:04.170
and you don't want that to take place.

00:45:04.170 --> 00:45:05.400
And you tell 'em even,

00:45:05.400 --> 00:45:07.890
it's not cost effective to the company.

00:45:07.890 --> 00:45:09.993
Even if they only
understand economic state,

00:45:09.993 --> 00:45:12.063
make the conversation economical.

00:45:13.110 --> 00:45:14.520
- I think that's really wise.

00:45:14.520 --> 00:45:15.960
And I would say even a step further,

00:45:15.960 --> 00:45:18.450
one thing that I've noticed
and been blessed is to actually

00:45:18.450 --> 00:45:20.790
have leaders sometimes
take that initiative.

00:45:20.790 --> 00:45:23.647
So even stepping into new
roles and having leaders say,

00:45:23.647 --> 00:45:26.370
"Do you need me to talk to
the next supervisor in line."

00:45:26.370 --> 00:45:28.140
To ask them to remove
something from your plate

00:45:28.140 --> 00:45:30.990
so that you can do this
effectively and not be spread thin.

00:45:30.990 --> 00:45:32.190
So I a hundred percent agree.

00:45:32.190 --> 00:45:33.270
And then from both sides,

00:45:33.270 --> 00:45:34.680
that's been really
powerful to see as well.

00:45:34.680 --> 00:45:36.005
- Yeah.

00:45:36.005 --> 00:45:37.676
And you feel supported when that happens.

00:45:37.676 --> 00:45:40.920
And it makes you enjoy the workplace

00:45:40.920 --> 00:45:43.170
and the workplace satisfaction increases

00:45:43.170 --> 00:45:45.540
because you see that people
are genuinely concerned

00:45:45.540 --> 00:45:47.160
about you and your wellbeing,

00:45:47.160 --> 00:45:49.950
and not just about the productivity

00:45:49.950 --> 00:45:51.750
or your talent, so to speak.

00:45:51.750 --> 00:45:52.583
- Yeah, yeah.

00:45:52.583 --> 00:45:55.200
And again, it seems to just
speak to the culture as well

00:45:55.200 --> 00:45:57.210
of the environment or the institution

00:45:57.210 --> 00:45:59.280
and really valuing people.

00:45:59.280 --> 00:46:00.130
- That's so true.

00:46:02.160 --> 00:46:04.680
- Well you've shared a lot
of just practical information

00:46:04.680 --> 00:46:06.900
that's been really useful
and perspectives as well,

00:46:06.900 --> 00:46:07.733
but I'm curious,

00:46:07.733 --> 00:46:10.170
are there any last words
you'd really wanna share

00:46:10.170 --> 00:46:11.670
with the listeners to encourage, again,

00:46:11.670 --> 00:46:14.130
many who are listening
who are in service roles

00:46:14.130 --> 00:46:18.780
or even in teams and trying
to meet the needs of those

00:46:18.780 --> 00:46:20.490
in their team, and just
all these different topics.

00:46:20.490 --> 00:46:22.350
Anything you'd wanna leave them with?

00:46:22.350 --> 00:46:23.340
- I would just encourage 'em

00:46:23.340 --> 00:46:25.110
to look at the dashboard of their soul

00:46:25.110 --> 00:46:27.600
and to see where they are,

00:46:27.600 --> 00:46:30.810
and check on your dashboard often.

00:46:30.810 --> 00:46:35.010
Is there an increase of
cynicism? That's bad.

00:46:35.010 --> 00:46:38.490
If there's a decrease of
creativity, that's bad.

00:46:38.490 --> 00:46:41.970
If there's an increase
of empathy, that's good.

00:46:41.970 --> 00:46:44.550
A decrease of productivity, that's bad.

00:46:44.550 --> 00:46:46.050
So you look at some of the things

00:46:46.050 --> 00:46:48.180
on the dashboard of your life,

00:46:48.180 --> 00:46:50.640
and it'll help you see where you are.

00:46:50.640 --> 00:46:52.950
And when you see things that are assigned

00:46:52.950 --> 00:46:54.540
that are indicators and
say, "Wait a second,

00:46:54.540 --> 00:46:55.890
that's not good."

00:46:55.890 --> 00:46:58.590
Take some time for yourself and recharge.

00:46:58.590 --> 00:47:00.327
- It's really well said. Really well said.

00:47:00.327 --> 00:47:02.550
And I know that people are
gonna listen to this episode

00:47:02.550 --> 00:47:04.560
more than once just to
soak in all the things

00:47:04.560 --> 00:47:06.510
that you and Chris Pfund have mentioned.

00:47:06.510 --> 00:47:08.790
And it's been a pleasure
to see the overlap

00:47:08.790 --> 00:47:09.690
and for both of you,

00:47:09.690 --> 00:47:12.840
just to see the ways that
you're really incorporating this

00:47:12.840 --> 00:47:14.670
in who you are and how you're leading,

00:47:14.670 --> 00:47:15.600
and how you're modeling,

00:47:15.600 --> 00:47:18.210
and how you're also encouraging
others to do the same.

00:47:18.210 --> 00:47:20.670
And so I'm hopeful that
there'll be a ripple effect

00:47:20.670 --> 00:47:22.470
